codex
ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: red
EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [
  {system: "vLLM", url: "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm", overlap: "No substantive overlap; inference/runtime system. Search surfaced n-gram speculative decoding docs, not corpus contamination detection."},
  {system: "SGLang", url: "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang", overlap: "No substantive overlap; serving/runtime benchmarks, not training-corpus benchmark contamination measurement."},
  {system: "LMCache", url: "https://github.com/LMCache/LMCache", overlap: "No substantive overlap; KV-cache/runtime system, not eval contamination detection."},
  {system: "FlashInfer", url: "https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer", overlap: "No substantive overlap; inference kernels, not corpus contamination measurement."},
  {system: "TensorRT-LLM", url: "https://github.com/NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM", overlap: "No substantive overlap; ngram examples are decoding/runtime mechanisms, not dataset contamination checks."},
  {system: "GPT-3 contamination analysis", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165", overlap: "High overlap on passage/n-gram contamination detection: GPT-3 defines dirty examples by any N-gram overlap with training documents, min 8/max 13 words, and computes exact collisions with Apache Spark."},
  {system: "PaLM contamination analysis", url: "https://jmlr.org/papers/v24/22-1144.html", overlap: "High overlap on benchmark contamination measurement and precision caveat: PaLM discusses high-order n-gram overlap, clean subsets, and false positives from web context without unfair answer leakage."},
  {system: "google-research/deduplicate-text-datasets / Lee et al. 2021", url: "https://github.com/google-research/deduplicate-text-datasets", overlap: "High overlap on exact substring dedup and approximate full-document MinHash; already separates partial substring duplication from document-level near-duplicate matching."},
  {system: "Open-Source Data Contamination Report", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17589", overlap: "Moderate overlap: open pipeline reports benchmark contamination rates via search/Common Crawl and verbatim overlap, with 1%-45%+ contamination ranges, but does not appear to isolate doc-level MinHash undercount vs passage matching."}
]
CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: integration-only
SCALE_REALISM: Does not hold as a systems artifact yet. The mechanism is algebraically expected from Jaccard union normalization, and L0 uses N=300 docs/M=60 items with distinct benchmark vocabulary, synthetic embedding, lexical 8-grams, serial CPU, and no real MinHash-LSH/index cost. It does not demonstrate behavior on C4/Pile/Dolma shards, real benchmark strings, boilerplate-heavy web text, benchmark item variants, URL/source leakage, or distributed retrieval scale. The precision boundary is useful but still synthetic; PaLM already documents that high-order n-gram overlap can overcount contexts that do not constitute unfair contamination.
RATIONALE: The named OSS inference systems do not already ship this mechanism, but that is because they are the wrong class of system. The relevant existing systems/papers already implement the key components: GPT-3 uses exact high-order N-gram train/test overlap and marks examples dirty on any collision; PaLM uses benchmark overlap analysis and manual contamination classification; Lee et al. provide both exact substring and approximate MinHash dedup tooling. The packet’s measured miss gap mainly combines known document-level near-duplicate dedup with known passage/n-gram contamination checks and shows the expected containment-vs-Jaccard failure on a toy setup. I see insufficient evidence for a systems insight or design beyond gluing established detectors into a synthetic comparison. Prompt version: v001.
